Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/5] proc: show CPUs of other machines and the work done on them (dnuma view)

dnuma (https://github.com/adamdeprince/medusa) runs the user code of
ordinary, unmodified processes on the CPUs of other machines. The process
itself never leaves: it keeps its pid, files, terminal and place in the
process tree on the machine it was started on (the master), which executes
every system call for it, and it sleeps there while its code runs
elsewhere. All of that is done in user space with ptrace, userfaultfd and
RDMA, on stock kernels.

What user space cannot do is make this visible. top shows such a process
as idle, and the CPUs it is using are not listed anywhere. This series
lets the manager describe both to the master's kernel, which then shows
them in the files tools already read:

  /proc/cpuinfo                   remote CPUs listed after the local ones
  /proc/stat                      a cpuN line for each, counted in "cpu"
  /proc/<pid>/stat and status     remote CPU, running state, CPU time
  /sys/devices/system/cpu/online  remote CPUs too, if asked (patch 5)

It is display only. No CPU mask, scheduler structure or accounting field
is touched; remote CPUs are numbered from nr_cpu_ids up; every hook is
behind one static key that is off unless a manager has the control file
open; and what a manager wrote is dropped when it closes the file or dies.

This is an RFC and is not proposed for merging in this form. Listing CPUs
the scheduler does not have is acceptable for a system that is explicit
about it, not obviously as a general kernel feature, and patch 5 changes
what the C library reports as the number of online processors. Patch 5 is
last so that it can be left out; dnuma-master detects its absence.

Patches:

  1  the control file and the state behind it (nothing shown yet)
  2  /proc/cpuinfo
  3  /proc/stat
  4  /proc/<pid>/stat and /proc/<pid>/status
  5  /sys/devices/system/cpu/online (optional)

Applies to v7.3.5. CONFIG_DNUMA_VIEW, x86-64, default off.

Origin of the code. The patches, their changelogs and the tests were
written by an AI coding assistant (Claude) working at the direction of
the submitter, who should be asked about intent and design. The commits
carry Assisted-by tags and no Signed-off-by: nobody has yet certified the
Developer's Certificate of Origin for them.

Testing.

  - Each commit builds without warnings (W=1 for the touched directories)
    with CONFIG_DNUMA_VIEW on and off.
  - A 4-CPU KVM guest boots the result with a test as init (kernel/test in
    the dnuma repository): 53 checks of the control file, /proc/cpuinfo,
    /proc/stat, /proc/<pid>/stat and status, sysfs "online", the ps and
    top binaries of procps 3.3.17, 1500 rounds of concurrent readers
    against a writer adding and removing CPUs, and cleanup on close. They
    pass on a production configuration and on one with PROVE_LOCKING,
    PROVE_RCU, KASAN, DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP and DEBUG_LIST, with nothing in
    the kernel log.
  - On real hardware: a 96-CPU master running this kernel with one
    machine attached that offers 24 CPUs. dnuma's test suite passes there,
    including a test that follows the chain from the other machine's
    counters to what top prints.

Not tested: architectures other than x86-64, CPU hotplug or suspend while
a view exists, long uptimes, and the series with patch 5 left out (it
builds; it has not been run).

Adam DePrince (5):
  proc: add /proc/dnuma_view, a description of CPUs and work on other
    machines
  proc: list CPUs of other machines in /proc/cpuinfo
  proc: show CPUs of other machines in /proc/stat
  proc: show where a task executing on another machine runs and what it
    uses
  cpu: optionally list CPUs of other machines in sysfs "online"

